<!-- SHOFAR MODAL -->
<div id="soundModal" class="modal">
  <div class="modal-content">
    <button class="modal-close" onclick="closeSoundModal()">&times;</button>
    <div class="modal-title">Awaken the Soul: The Science of the Shofar</div>
    <div class="modal-section">
      <div class="modal-section-title">The Acoustic Journey</div>
      <div class="modal-text">The shofar blast is not merely sound — it is a deliberate acoustic intervention designed to penetrate consciousness and trigger profound internal transformation.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="modal-section">
      <div class="modal-section-title">Frequency &amp; Neural Activation</div>
      <div class="frequency-note">
        <strong>The shofar's primary blast (tekiah) resonates at frequencies between 440–800 Hz.</strong> These frequencies engage both the auditory cortex and the limbic system — the emotional and memory centers of the brain. The tonal complexity activates bilateral hemispheric engagement, creating a synchronized state primed for introspection.
      </div>
      <div class="modal-text">Modern acoustic research shows that frequencies in this range stimulate the vagus nerve, activating the parasympathetic nervous system (rest-and-digest mode) while simultaneously heightening alertness. This paradoxical state — calm yet attentive — is precisely the condition required for genuine spiritual awareness.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="modal-section">
      <div class="modal-section-title">Sound Therapy Principles</div>
      <div class="modal-text"><strong>Brainwave Entrainment:</strong> Repetitive acoustic stimuli at specific frequencies encourage the brain to synchronize. The broken blasts of the shofar (shevarim and teruah) create rhythmic patterns that guide the mind toward theta and alpha brainwave states — associated with meditation, insight, and emotional processing.</div>
      <div class="modal-text"><strong>Vagal Tone &amp; Resilience:</strong> Sound therapy strengthens vagal tone — the capacity of the vagus nerve to regulate heart rate, breath, and stress response. A stronger vagal tone correlates with psychological resilience, emotional regulation, and the ability to respond thoughtfully to crisis.</div>
      <div class="modal-text"><strong>Somatic Memory Clearing:</strong> Deep, unexpected acoustic stimuli can interrupt habitual neural patterns and surface suppressed memories and emotions. This is why the shofar often provokes tears, silence, or profound stillness — the sound is literally rewiring conscious awareness.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="modal-section">
      <div class="modal-section-title">Ancient Wisdom, Modern Science</div>
      <div class="modal-text">The Jewish tradition teaches that the shofar call serves teshuvah — "return" — a complete turning toward truth. Neuroscience confirms that acoustic stimuli of this particular frequency profile create optimal conditions for cognitive and emotional reorientation.</div>
      <div class="modal-text">When the shofar sounds, you are not merely hearing a noise. Your body is being invited into a state of profound attentiveness. Your nervous system is being recalibrated. Your capacity for presence is being awakened.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="modal-section" style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding-top:16px;margin-top:20px;">
      <div class="modal-text" style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ink-mut);font-style:italic;">This synthesis draws on research from neuroscience, acoustic therapy (Beaulieu, 2010), polyvagal theory (Porges, 2011), and Jewish contemplative traditions spanning millennia.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- COLOGNE MODAL -->
<div id="cologneModal" class="modal">
  <div class="modal-content">
    <button class="modal-close" onclick="closeCologneModal()">&times;</button>
    <div class="modal-title">Cologne · A Testament to Survival</div>
    <div class="modal-section">
      <div class="modal-section-title">Köln, Germany</div>
      <div class="modal-text">Cologne (Köln in German) is a medieval city on the Rhine River in western Germany, known for its soaring Gothic cathedral and vibrant culture. For centuries, it was home to a thriving Jewish community.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="modal-section">
      <div class="modal-section-title">During the Holocaust</div>
      <div class="modal-text">As Nazi persecution escalated, Jews in Cologne were forced into hiding. Many found refuge in cellars, attics, and concealed rooms throughout the city — places where they could survive, but not truly live.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="modal-section">
      <div class="modal-section-title">The Inscription</div>
      <div class="modal-text">On the wall of one such cellar — a hiding place where Jewish families cowered in darkness during the height of the terror — someone wrote a prayer. It was not written in anger or despair, but in faith:</div>
      <div class="scripture" style="margin-top:12px;">
        <em>"I believe in the sun even when it is not shining.<br>
        I believe in love even when not feeling it.<br>
        I believe in God even when He is silent."</em>
      </div>
      <div class="modal-text" style="margin-top:12px;">This inscription became one of the most profound testaments to human resilience ever recorded. It survives as proof that even in the depths of humanity's darkest hour, faith endured.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="modal-section" style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding-top:16px;margin-top:20px;">
      <div class="modal-text" style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ink-mut);font-style:italic;">This prayer reminds us that belief is not dependent on feeling. Truth does not depend on comfort. Freedom must be defended not in good times, but especially in the darkest of times.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

